Older systems like Hadoop MapReduce achieved fault tolerance through brute force: they copied every piece of data to three different hard drives. 
Apache Spark is much more elegant. Instead of replicating the data itself, Spark remembers the exact recipe used to create the data,
allowing it to instantly reconstruct anything that gets lost.

Technical Breakdown :-

Immutability: Once an RDD or DataFrame is created in memory, it can never be changed. Transformations only create new, child datasets.

The Lineage Graph: Spark records every transformation (filter, map, join) applied to the base data in a Directed Acyclic Graph.
If a worker node suddenly catches fire and loses its slice of the data (say, Partition 4) from RAM, the Driver doesn't crash the job.
It simply consults the Lineage Graph, finds the exact sequence of transformations required to build Partition 4, goes back to the original source data on disk,
and assigns a surviving worker node to recompute just that missing slice.

Business Context :-

By eliminating the need to constantly write replicated data to physical disk over the network, Spark achieves its signature in-memory speed.
This design allows businesses to run massive data pipelines on cheap, ephemeral cloud infrastructure (like AWS Spot Instances). 
If a cheap node is preempted and dies mid-job, the pipeline transparently heals itself without human intervention.

Design Decision :-

However, this 'recomputation' superpower has an Achilles' heel: incredibly long lineages. If an iterative machine learning pipeline has a lineage of 500 transformations,
losing a node at step 499 means Spark has to recompute everything from step 1,
which could take hours. As a data engineer, I proactively break these massive DAGs by explicitly calling .checkpoint() or
writing the intermediate state to a Delta Lake table. 
This physically writes the data to reliable storage (like S3), truncating the DAG and establishing a safe 'save point' for rapid recovery.
